            <ArticleTitle>Idiopathic ovarian vein thrombosis in a nonperipartum patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1468-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/AOG.0000000000000648</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Ovarian vein thrombosis is a rare diagnosis typically seen in the early peripartum period but also in other thrombophilic states such as postsurgery, pelvic inflammatory disease, malignancy, or sepsis. We describe a case of idiopathic ovarian vein thrombosis in a healthy woman far outside the peripartum window.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">The patient is a 29-year-old woman, gravida 3 para 2102, with no significant surgical or medical history referred for 8 months of severe left lower quadrant pain. An ultrasonogram revealed a nonocclusive left ovarian vein thrombosis. Hypercoagulable workup and all other laboratory tests were normal. The thrombus resolved within 2 months of starting oral anticoagulation therapy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This case demonstrates the importance of including idiopathic ovarian vein thrombosis in the differential diagnosis of nonperipartum females with pelvic pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
